.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OPENSSL-QUIC-CONCURRENCY 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openssl-quic-concurrency
.Nd OpenSSL QUIC Concurrency Model
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A QUIC domain is a group of QUIC resources such as listeners (see
.Xr SSL_new_listener 3 )
and connections which share common event processing resources, such as
internal pollers, timers and locks.
All usage of OpenSSL QUIC happens inside a QUIC domain.
.Pp
These resources can be accessed and used concurrently depending on the
circumstances.
This man page discusses the available concurrency models and how they
can be used.
.Sh EXPLICIT AND IMPLICIT QUIC DOMAINS
A QUIC domain is instantiated either explicitly
.Pf ( Xr SSL_new_domain 3 )
or implicitly by calling
.Xr SSL_new 3
or
.Xr SSL_new_listener 3 :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It
An explicit QUIC domain is created by and visible to the application as
a QUIC domain SSL object and has other QUIC SSL objects created
underneath it, such as listeners or connections.
.It
An implicit QUIC domain is one which is created internally due to the
direct creation of a QUIC connection or listener SSL object; the
application does not explicitly create a QUIC domain SSL object and
never directly references the domain.
.El
.Pp
Explicit creation of a QUIC domain provides the greatest level of
control for an application.
Applications can use an implicit QUIC domain for ease of use and to
avoid needing to create a separate QUIC domain SSL object.
.Pp
Regardless of whether a QUIC domain is explicitly created, the internal
processing model is the same and the application must choose an
appropriate concurrency model as discussed below.
.Sh CONCURRENCY MODELS
The OpenSSL QUIC implementation supports multiple concurrency models to
support a wide variety of usage scenarios.
.Pp
The available concurrency models are as follows:
.Bl -bullet
.It
The
.Sy Single-Threaded Concurrency Model (SCM) ,
which supports only application-synchronised single-threaded usage.
.It
The
.Sy Contentive Concurrency Model (CCM) ,
which supports multi-threaded usage.
.It
The
.Sy Thread-Assisted Concurrency Model (TACM) ,
which also supports multi-threaded usage and provides assistance to an
application for handling QUIC timer events.
.El
.Pp
The merits of these models are as follows:
.Bl -bullet
.It
The
.Sy Single-Threaded Concurrency Model (SCM)
performs no locking or synchronisation.
It is entirely up to the application to synchronise access to the QUIC
domain and its subsidiary SSL objects.
.Pp
This concurrency model is also useful for an application which wants to
use the OpenSSL QUIC implementation as a pure state machine.
.It
The
.Sy Contentive Concurrency Model (CCM)
performs automatic locking when making API calls to SSL objects in a
QUIC domain.
This provides automatic synchronisation for multi-threaded usage of QUIC
objects.
For example, different QUIC stream SSL objects in the same QUIC
connection can be safely accessed from different threads.
.Pp
This concurrency model adds the overhead of locking over the
Single-Threaded Concurrency Model in order to support multi-threaded
usage, but provides limited performance in highly contended
multi-threaded usage due to its simple approach.
However, it may still prove a good solution for a broad class of
applications which spend the majority of their time in application logic
and not in QUIC I/O processing.
.Pp
An advantage of this model relative to the more sophisticated
concurrency models below is that it does not create any OS threads.
.It
The
.Sy Thread-Assisted Concurrency Model (TACM)
is identical to the Contentive Concurrency Model except that a thread is
spun up in the background to ensure that QUIC timer events are handled
in a timely fashion.
This ensures that QUIC timeout events are handled even if an application
does not periodically call into the QUIC domain to ensure that any
outstanding QUIC-related timer or network I/O events are handled.
The assist thread contends for the same resources like any other thread.
However, handshake layer events (TLS) are never processed by the assist
thread.
.El
.Pp
The default concurrency model is CCM or TACM, depending on the
.Sy SSL_METHOD
used with a
.Sy SSL_CTX .
Using
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_method 3
results in a default concurrency model of CCM, whereas using
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_thread_method 3
results in a default concurrency model of TACM.
.Pp
Additional concurrency models may be offered in future releases of
OpenSSL.
.Sh BLOCKING I/O CAPABILITIES
All of the supported concurrency models are capable of supporting
blocking I/O calls, where application-level I/O calls (for example, to
.Xr SSL_read_ex 3
or
.Xr SSL_write_ex 3
on a QUIC stream SSL object) block until the request can be serviced.
This includes the use of
.Xr SSL_poll 3
in a blocking fashion.
.Pp
Supporting blocking API calls reliably with multi-threaded usage
requires the creation of additional OS resources such as internal file
descriptors to allow threads to be woken when necessary.
This creation of internal OS resources is optional and may need to be
explicitly requested by an application depending on the chosen
concurrency model.
If this functionality is disabled, depending on the chosen concurrency
model, blocking API calls may not be available and calls to
.Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3
attempting to enable blocking mode may fail, notwithstanding the
following section.
.Ss Legacy Blocking Support Compatibility
OpenSSL 3.2 and 3.3 contained a buggy implementation of blocking QUIC
I/O calls which is only reliable under single-threaded usage.
This functionality is always available in the Single-Threaded
Concurrency Model (SCM), where it works reliably.
.Pp
For compatibility reasons, this functionality is also available under
the default concurrency model if the application does not explicitly
specify a concurrency model or disable it.
This is known as Legacy Blocking Compatibility Mode, and its usage is
not recommended for multi-threaded applications.
.Sh RECOMMENDED USAGE
New applications are advised to choose a concurrency model as follows:
.Bl -bullet
.It
A purely single-threaded application, or an application which wishes to
use OpenSSL QUIC as a state machine and manage synchronisation itself,
should explicitly select the SCM concurrency model.
.It
An application which wants to engage in multi-threaded usage of
different QUIC connections or streams in the same QUIC domain should a)
select the CCM or TACM concurrency model and b) explicitly opt in or out
of blocking I/O support (depending on whether the application wishes to
make blocking I/O calls), disabling Legacy Blocking Compatibility Mode.
.Pp
An application should select the CCM concurrency model if the
application can guarantee that a QUIC domain will be serviced regularly
(for example, because the application can guarantee that the timeout
returned by
.Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3
will be handled).
If an application is unable to do this, it should select the TACM
concurrency model.
.It
Applications should explicitly configure a concurrency model during
initialisation.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURING A CONCURRENCY MODEL
If using an explicit QUIC domain, a concurrency model is chosen when
calling
.Xr SSL_new_domain 3
by specifying zero or more of the following flags:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_SINGLE_THREAD
Specifying this flag configures the Single-Threaded Concurrency Model
(SCM).
.It Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_MULTI_THREAD
Speciyfing this flag configures the Contentive Concurrency Model (CCM)
(unless
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_THREAD_ASSISTED
is also specified).
.It Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_THREAD_ASSISTED
Specifying this flag configures the Thread-Assisted Concurrency Model
(TACM). It implies
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_MULTI_THREAD .
.It Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_BLOCKING
Enable reliable support for blocking I/O calls, allocating whatever OS
resources are necessary to realise this.
If this flag is specified,
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_LEGACY_BLOCKING
is ignored.
.Pp
Details on the allocated OS resources can be found under
.Sx CONSUMPTION OF OS
RESOURCES
below.
.It Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_LEGACY_BLOCKING
Enables legacy blocking compatibility mode.
See
.Sx Legacy Blocking Support
Compatibility .
.El
.Pp
Mutually exclusive flag combinations result in an error (for example,
combining
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_SINGLE_THREAD
and
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_MULTI_THREADED Ns ).
.Pp
The concurrency model for a domain cannot be changed after the domain is
created.
.Ss Default Behaviour
If none of
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_SINGLE_THREAD ,
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_MULTI_THREAD
or
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_THREAD_ASSISTED
are provided to
.Xr SSL_new_domain 3
or another constructor function which can accept the above flags, the
default concurrency model set on the
.Sy SSL_CTX
is used.
This default can be set and get using
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_domain_flags 3
and
.Xr SSL_CTX_get_domain_flags 3 .
Any additional flags provided (for example,
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_BLOCCKING )
are added to the set of inherited flags.
.Pp
The default concurrency model set on a newly created
.Sy SSL_CTX
is determined as follows:
.Bl -bullet
.It
If an
.Sy SSL_METHOD
of
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_thread_method 3
is used, the Thread-Assisted Concurrency Model (TACM) is used with the
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_BLOCKING
flag.
This provides reliable blocking functionality.
.It
Otherwise, if OpenSSL was built without threading support, the
Single-Threaded Concurrency Model (SCM) is used, with the
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_LEGACY_BLOCKING
flag.
.It
Otherwise, if an
.Sy SSL_METHOD
of
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_method 3
is used, the Contentive Concurrency Model (CCM) is used with the
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_LEGACY_BLOCKING
flag.
.It
Otherwise, the Contentive Concurrency Model (CCM) is used.
.El
.Pp
The default concurrency model may vary between releases of OpenSSL. An
application may specify one or more of the domain flags above to ensure
consistent usage of a specific concurrency model between releases.
.Ss Configuration of Concurrency Models with Implicit QUIC Domains
If an explicit QUIC domain is not explicitly created using
.Xr SSL_new_domain 3 ,
an implicit QUIC domain is created when calling
.Xr SSL_new_listener 3
or
.Xr SSL_new 3 .
Such a domain will use the default domain flags configured on the
.Sy SSL_CTX
as described above.
.Sh CONSUMPTION OF OS RESOURCES
If full blocking I/O support is selected using
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_BLOCKING ,
at least one socket, socket-like OS handle or file descriptor must be
allocated to allow one thread to wake other threads which may be
blocking in calls to OS socket polling interfaces such as select(2) or
poll(2). This is allocated automatically internally by OpenSSL.
.Pp
If the Thread-Assisted Concurrency Model (TACM) is selected, a
background thread is spawned.
This also implies
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_BLOCKING
and the above.
.Pp
The internal consumption by OpenSSL of mutexes, condition variables,
spin locks or other similar thread synchronisation primitives is
unspecified under all concurrency models.
.Pp
The internal consumption by OpenSSL of threads is unspecified under the
Thread-Assisted Concurrency Model.
.Pp
The internal consumption by OpenSSL of sockets, socket-like OS handles
or file descriptors, or other resources as needed to support
inter-thread notification, is unspecified under the Thread-Assisted
Concurrency Model or when using
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_BLOCKING .
.Sh BEHAVIOUR OF SSL OBJECTS
A QUIC SSL object has blocking mode enabled by default where
.Sy all
of the following criteria are met:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_BLOCKING
or
.Sy SSL_DOMAIN_FLAG_LEGACY_BLOCKING
is enabled; and
.It
The QUIC connection is being used with network read and write BIOs which
expose supported poll descriptors.
See
.Xr openssl-quic 7
for details.
.El
.Pp
In all other cases, a QUIC SSL object has blocking mode disabled by
default.
The blocking mode can be changed explicitly using
.Xr SSL_set_blocking_mode 3 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl-quic 7 ,
.Xr SSL_handle_events 3 ,
.Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3 ,
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_thread_method 3 ,
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_domain_flags 3 ,
.Xr SSL_new_domain 3
